<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements of the `atlas` CLI (TUI, MCP server, `atlas hostd`, `atlas crew`) in the Developer ID build:
  release.yml signs it with the runtime option, a secure timestamp and this file as entitlements. The hardened runtime needs
  none of the exceptions for it, so the dictionary is empty ON PURPOSE: passing the file keeps the entitlement set an
  explicit, reviewed decision (a key added here shows up in a diff) instead of whatever codesign defaults to.

  Why nothing is needed, from the code and from running a hardened build (docs/release.md, "What is signed"):
  - No JIT, no wasm, no scripting engine, no executable memory tricks: no allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
  - It links only system libraries and loads no dylib at runtime: no disable-library-validation.
  - hostd's children (shells, agents, git, the crew helpers) are separate processes. The hardened runtime constrains
    the signed process only, and DYLD_* is never needed by atlas itself: no allow-dyld-environment-variables.
  - The phone gateway (iroh/QUIC listener on all interfaces once a phone is paired) is a plain socket: network
    entitlements belong to the App Sandbox, which the CLI does not use. The Developer ID signature is what makes the
    application firewall remember "allow incoming connections" across updates.
  - It never records the screen or the microphone (the app does, through its own entitlement), reads no Contacts,
    Calendar or Photos, and sends no Apple Events.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
